{#
  How the page is put together, independent of which template drew the header.

  A template owns the top of the page -- the masthead, the band, the side
  column -- and that is where the eight of them differ from each other. Below
  the header they were all the same document: a heading, then its entries,
  then the next heading. Six named looks over eight templates therefore came
  out as one design in six colours, which is exactly what it looked like.

  This file is the other axis. It restyles the body of the page -- where the
  section name sits, how an entry is separated from the one above it, what
  carries the eye down the column -- and it composes with every template, so
  the two together make thirty-two page designs rather than eight.

  **Scope.** Only the sections in the main flow: direct children of the sheet
  in a single-column template, direct children of ``.main`` in a two-column
  one. A 54mm side column has no room for a gutter or a date rail, and the
  templates that have one already style it themselves.

  **Specificity, not ``!important``.** Every rule here is prefixed with
  ``body`` so it scores above the ``.sec > h2`` in a template's own block, and
  this file is included after that block so equal scores still fall this way.
  An ``!important`` would win too, and would also make the next override
  impossible.
#}

{% if design.layout == 'gutter' %}
/* Gutter: the section name moves into a column of its own down the left, and
   the reading matter gets one straight edge to run down. The grid is what
   does it -- not a float and not a table -- so the markup order is still
   heading, then content, and a parser reads one sensible column. */
body .sheet > .sec,
body .main > .sec {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 26mm 1fr;
  gap: 0 6mm;
}
body .sheet > .sec > h2,
body .main > .sec > h2 {
  grid-column: 1;
  margin: 1pt 0 0;
  padding: 0;
  border: none;
  background: none;
  text-align: left;
  text-indent: 0;
  font-family: var(--f-body);
  font-size: calc(var(--fs) * .78);
  font-weight: 700;
  letter-spacing: .09em;
  /* The column is sized for the longest label there is. Breaking a word
     inside it would read as a typo, so this opts out of the document-wide
     break-anywhere rule and lets a long one wrap instead. */
  overflow-wrap: normal;
  word-break: keep-all;
}
body .sheet > .sec > :not(h2),
body .main > .sec > :not(h2) { grid-column: 2; }
/* A skills section set in columns fights a grid: the column rule spans the
   whole section, gutter included. */
body .sheet > .sec-skills,
body .main > .sec-skills { column-count: 1; }
{% endif %}

{% if design.layout == 'rail' %}
/* A template that sets its own body -- Minimalist's gutter grid -- has to be
   put back to a plain column first, or the heading is styled inside a 30mm
   cell and the band ends up the width of the word in it. `stacked` is the
   layout that leaves a template's own arrangement alone; this is not it. */
body .sheet > .sec,
body .main > .sec { display: block; }

/* Rail: a hairline runs down the left of each section with a node at every
   entry, so the eye is given a spine to follow instead of counting bold
   lines. The nodes are drawn, not typed -- nothing here reaches the text
   layer a parser reads. */
body .sheet > .sec > h2,
body .main > .sec > h2 {
  border: none;
  background: none;
  padding: 0 0 0 7pt;
  text-align: left;
  text-indent: 0;
  border-left: 2.5pt solid var(--accent);
  margin-bottom: 6pt;
}
body .sheet > .sec .entry,
body .main > .sec .entry {
  position: relative;
  padding-left: 13pt;
  margin-top: 9pt;
}
body .sheet > .sec .entry::before,
body .main > .sec .entry::before {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  left: 0;
  /* Half a line down, so the node sits on the title rather than above it. */
  top: calc(var(--fs) * var(--lh) / 2 - 2.4pt);
  width: 4.8pt;
  height: 4.8pt;
  border-radius: 50%;
  background: var(--accent);
}
body .sheet > .sec .entry::after,
body .main > .sec .entry::after {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  left: 2.1pt;
  top: calc(var(--fs) * var(--lh) / 2 + 4pt);
  /* Down to the next node rather than to the end of this entry: the gap
     between two entries is the part of the spine that has to be continuous. */
  bottom: -9pt;
  width: .7pt;
  /* The accent at a quarter, not the rule grey: --rule is #D7D7DE, and less
     than a point of it on white is a line you have to be told is there. */
  background: var(--accent);
  opacity: .26;
}
/* The last entry's line would run past the section into the next heading. */
body .sheet > .sec .entry:last-of-type::after,
body .main > .sec .entry:last-of-type::after { display: none; }
{% endif %}

{% if design.layout == 'panel' %}
body .sheet > .sec,
body .main > .sec { display: block; }

/* Panel: each heading sits in a tinted band the width of the column, with the
   accent standing at its left edge. The tint is mixed against white rather
   than being a second colour to choose, so it is the accent at ten percent
   whatever the accent is -- and it prints as a pale grey in the greyscale a
   recruiter's office printer produces. */
body .sheet > .sec > h2,
body .main > .sec > h2 {
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 11%, #fff);
  border: none;
  border-left: 2.5pt solid var(--accent);
  padding: 3.2pt 7pt;
  margin-bottom: 6.5pt;
  text-align: left;
  text-indent: 0;
}
body .sheet > .sec > h2::after,
body .main > .sec > h2::after { display: none; }
/* A hairline between entries rather than only space: the band above has
   already made the section boundaries loud, and without it the entries inside
   one run together. */
body .sheet > .sec .entry + .entry,
body .main > .sec .entry + .entry {
  border-top: .5pt solid var(--rule-soft);
  padding-top: 6pt;
  margin-top: 6pt;
}
{% endif %}
